@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1018 = private unnamed_addr constant [937 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of the builtin `eval()` function.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `eval()` function is insecure as it enables arbitrary code execution.\0A\0AIf you need to evaluate an expression from a string, consider using\0A`ast.literal_eval()` instead, which will raise an exception if the\0Aexpression is not a valid Python literal.\0A\0AIn [preview], this rule will also flag references to `eval`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Ax = eval(input(\22Enter a number: \22))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom ast import literal_eval\0A\0Ax = literal_eval(input(\22Enter a number: \22))\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `eval`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#eval)\0A- [Python documentation: `literal_eval`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\0A- [_Eval really is dangerous_ by Ned Batchelder](https://nedbatchelder.com/blog/201206/eval_really_is_dangerous.html)\0A\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A", align 1
