@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1029 = private unnamed_addr constant [41 x i8] c"suspicious-non-cryptographic-random-usage", align 1
@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1030 = private unnamed_addr constant [908 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of cryptographically weak pseudo-random number generators.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ACryptographically weak pseudo-random number generators are insecure, as they\0Aare easily predictable. This can allow an attacker to guess the generated\0Anumbers and compromise the security of the system.\0A\0AInstead, use a cryptographically secure pseudo-random number generator\0A(such as using the [`secrets` module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/secrets.html))\0Awhen generating random numbers for security purposes.\0A\0AIn [preview], this rule will also flag references to these generators.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport random\0A\0Arandom.randrange(10)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport secrets\0A\0Asecrets.randbelow(10)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `random` \E2\80\94 Generate pseudo-random numbers](https://docs.python.org/3/library/random.html)\0A\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A", align 1
