@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1097 = private unnamed_addr constant [1099 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `raise` statements in exception handlers that lack a `from`\0Aclause.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, `raise` can be used with or without an exception from which the\0Acurrent exception is derived. This is known as exception chaining. When\0Aprinting the stack trace, chained exceptions are displayed in such a way\0Aso as make it easier to trace the exception back to its root cause.\0A\0AWhen raising an exception from within an `except` clause, always include a\0A`from` clause to facilitate exception chaining. If the exception is not\0Achained, it will be difficult to trace the exception back to its root cause.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Atry:\0A    ...\0Aexcept FileNotFoundError:\0A    if ...:\0A        raise RuntimeError(\22...\22)\0A    else:\0A        raise UserWarning(\22...\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Atry:\0A    ...\0Aexcept FileNotFoundError as exc:\0A    if ...:\0A        raise RuntimeError(\22...\22) from None\0A    else:\0A        raise UserWarning(\22...\22) from exc\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `raise` statement](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/simple_stmts.html#the-raise-statement)\0A", align 1
