@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1144 = private unnamed_addr constant [1808 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `extra` keywords in logging statements that clash with\0A`LogRecord` attributes.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `logging` module provides a mechanism for passing additional values to\0Abe logged using the `extra` keyword argument. These values are then passed\0Ato the `LogRecord` constructor.\0A\0AProviding a value via `extra` that clashes with one of the attributes of\0Athe `LogRecord` constructor will raise a `KeyError` when the `LogRecord` is\0Aconstructed.\0A\0A## Known problems\0A\0AThis rule detects uses of the `logging` module via a heuristic.\0ASpecifically, it matches against:\0A\0A- Uses of the `logging` module itself (e.g., `import logging; logging.info(...)`).\0A- Uses of `flask.current_app.logger` (e.g., `from flask import current_app; current_app.logger.info(...)`).\0A- Objects whose name starts with `log` or ends with `logger` or `logging`,\0A  when used in the same file in which they are defined (e.g., `logger = logging.getLogger(); logger.info(...)`).\0A- Imported objects marked as loggers via the [`lint.logger-objects`] setting, which can be\0A  used to enforce these rules against shared logger objects (e.g., `from module import logger; logger.info(...)`,\0A  when [`lint.logger-objects`] is set to `[\22module.logger\22]`).\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0Alogging.basicConfig(format=\22%(name) - %(message)s\22, level=logging.INFO)\0A\0Ausername = \22Maria\22\0A\0Alogging.info(\22Something happened\22, extra=dict(name=username))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0Alogging.basicConfig(format=\22%(user_id)s - %(message)s\22, level=logging.INFO)\0A\0Ausername = \22Maria\22\0A\0Alogging.info(\22Something happened\22, extra=dict(user_id=username))\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.logger-objects`\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: LogRecord attributes](https://docs.python.org/3/library/logging.html#logrecord-attributes)\0A", align 1
