@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1158 = private unnamed_addr constant [982 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary `dict` kwargs.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf the `dict` keys are valid identifiers, they can be passed as keyword\0Aarguments directly.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Adef foo(bar):\0A    return bar + 1\0A\0A\0Aprint(foo(**{\22bar\22: 2}))  # prints 3\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Adef foo(bar):\0A    return bar + 1\0A\0A\0Aprint(foo(bar=2))  # prints 3\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe for dictionaries with comments interleaved between\0Athe items, as comments may be removed.\0A\0AFor example, the fix would be marked as unsafe in the following case:\0A\0A```python\0Afoo(\0A    **{\0A        # comment\0A        \22x\22: 1.0,\0A        # comment\0A        \22y\22: 2.0,\0A    }\0A)\0A```\0A\0Aas this is converted to `foo(x=1.0, y=2.0)` without any of the comments.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Dictionary displays](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#dictionary-displays)\0A- [Python documentation: Calls](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/expressions.html#calls)\0A", align 1
