@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1174 = private unnamed_addr constant [979 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for the presence of [PEP 484]-style positional-only parameters.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AHistorically, [PEP 484] recommended prefixing parameter names with double\0Aunderscores (`__`) to indicate to a type checker that they were\0Apositional-only. However, [PEP 570] (introduced in Python 3.8) introduced\0Adedicated syntax for positional-only arguments. If a forward slash (`/`) is\0Apresent in a function signature on Python 3.8+, all parameters prior to the\0Aslash are interpreted as positional-only.\0A\0AThe new syntax should be preferred as it is more widely used, more concise\0Aand more readable. It is also respected by Python at runtime, whereas the\0Aold-style syntax was only understood by type checkers.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```pyi\0Adef foo(__x: int) -> None: ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```pyi\0Adef foo(x: int, /) -> None: ...\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A\0A[PEP 484]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0484/#positional-only-arguments\0A[PEP 570]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0570\0A", align 1
