@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1178 = private unnamed_addr constant [1203 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for accesses on \22private\22 class members.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, the convention is such that class members that are prefixed\0Awith a single underscore, or prefixed but not suffixed with a double\0Aunderscore, are considered private and intended for internal use.\0A\0AUsing such \22private\22 members is considered a misuse of the class, as\0Athere are no guarantees that the member will be present in future\0Aversions, that it will have the same type, or that it will have the same\0Abehavior. Instead, use the class's public interface.\0A\0AThis rule ignores accesses on dunder methods (e.g., `__init__`) and sunder\0Amethods (e.g., `_missing_`).\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass Class:\0A    def __init__(self):\0A        self._private_member = \22...\22\0A\0A\0Avar = Class()\0Aprint(var._private_member)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass Class:\0A    def __init__(self):\0A        self.public_member = \22...\22\0A\0A\0Avar = Class()\0Aprint(var.public_member)\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.flake8-self.ignore-names`\0A\0A## References\0A- [_What is the meaning of single or double underscores before an object name?_](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1301346/what-is-the-meaning-of-single-and-double-underscore-before-an-object-name)\0A", align 1
