@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1339 = private unnamed_addr constant [1073 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `subprocess.Popen` with a `preexec_fn` argument.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `preexec_fn` argument is unsafe within threads as it can lead to\0Adeadlocks. Furthermore, `preexec_fn` is [targeted for deprecation].\0A\0AInstead, consider using task-specific arguments such as `env`,\0A`start_new_session`, and `process_group`. These are not prone to deadlocks\0Aand are more explicit.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport os, subprocess\0A\0Asubprocess.Popen(foo, preexec_fn=os.setsid)\0Asubprocess.Popen(bar, preexec_fn=os.setpgid(0, 0))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport subprocess\0A\0Asubprocess.Popen(foo, start_new_session=True)\0Asubprocess.Popen(bar, process_group=0)  # Introduced in Python 3.11\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `subprocess.Popen`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html#popen-constructor)\0A- [Why `preexec_fn` in `subprocess.Popen` may lead to deadlock?](https://discuss.python.org/t/why-preexec-fn-in-subprocess-popen-may-lead-to-deadlock/16908/2)\0A\0A[targeted for deprecation]: https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/82616\0A", align 1
