@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1370 = private unnamed_addr constant [782 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `subprocess.run` that set the `universal_newlines`\0Akeyword argument.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AAs of Python 3.7, the `universal_newlines` keyword argument has been\0Arenamed to `text`, and now exists for backwards compatibility. The\0A`universal_newlines` keyword argument may be removed in a future version of\0APython. Prefer `text`, which is more explicit and readable.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport subprocess\0A\0Asubprocess.run([\22foo\22], universal_newlines=True)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport subprocess\0A\0Asubprocess.run([\22foo\22], text=True)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python 3.7 release notes](https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.7.html#subprocess)\0A- [Python documentation: `subprocess.run`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html#subprocess.run)\0A", align 1
