@anon.158f56963078a041553d42a6ce1813d5.1411 = private unnamed_addr constant [1235 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of the `+` operator to concatenate collections.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, the `+` operator can be used to concatenate collections (e.g.,\0A`x + y` to concatenate the lists `x` and `y`).\0A\0AHowever, collections can be concatenated more efficiently using the\0Aunpacking operator (e.g., `[*x, *y]` to concatenate `x` and `y`).\0A\0APrefer the unpacking operator to concatenate collections, as it is more\0Areadable and flexible. The `*` operator can unpack any iterable, whereas\0A `+` operates only on particular sequences which, in many cases, must be of\0Athe same type.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afoo = [2, 3, 4]\0Abar = [1] + foo + [5, 6]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afoo = [2, 3, 4]\0Abar = [1, *foo, 5, 6]\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThe fix is always marked as unsafe because the `+` operator uses the `__add__` magic method and\0A`*`-unpacking uses the `__iter__` magic method. Both of these could have custom\0Aimplementations, causing the fix to change program behaviour.\0A\0A## References\0A- [PEP 448 \E2\80\93 Additional Unpacking Generalizations](https://peps.python.org/pep-0448/)\0A- [Python documentation: Sequence Types \E2\80\94 `list`, `tuple`, `range`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#sequence-types-list-tuple-range)\0A", align 1
